WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.350
What is the role of speaking?

00:00:01.350 --> 00:00:02.880
How important is speaking?

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:06.180
How can we improve our ability to speak?

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:10.260
And the reason I'm talking about it
is that, you know, wherever I go, if I

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:15.630
speak to people about language learning,
whether it was, uh, the group that

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:20.010
I met up with in Poland, one of the
participants was a teacher who said

00:00:20.010 --> 00:00:22.020
that her students are afraid to speak.

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:27.270
Uh, I met, uh, a few people in the,
in the lounge in Toronto Airport,

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:31.905
'cause our flight was delayed, and
this lady was saying that she had

00:00:31.905 --> 00:00:35.835
French in school and she understands,
but she's so afraid to speak.

00:00:35.835 --> 00:00:37.125
It just doesn't come out.

00:00:37.485 --> 00:00:40.275
Whenever she tries to speak, she fails.

00:00:40.755 --> 00:00:43.755
Uh, and I get these comments all
the time, people saying that,

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:46.875
you know, I understand, I can
read and, but I just can't speak.

00:00:47.135 --> 00:00:54.044
So kind of think about this and I, you
know, The first thing is obviously to

00:00:54.044 --> 00:00:55.544
speak well, you have to speak a lot.

00:00:55.905 --> 00:00:59.805
So if people are intimidated and
reluctant to speak, they will never

00:00:59.809 --> 00:01:01.275
improve their ability to speak.

00:01:01.815 --> 00:01:06.255
So the first thing is you have to get
over that hurdle, uh, that reluctance

00:01:06.255 --> 00:01:10.304
to speak, because ultimately, as
I say, you have to speak a lot.

00:01:10.545 --> 00:01:13.935
So the next question is, why is
it that people are afraid to speak

00:01:14.655 --> 00:01:16.485
but they're not afraid to listen?

00:01:16.545 --> 00:01:18.164
They're not afraid to read.

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:21.270
Maybe I don't like to
write 'cause I'm lazy.

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:25.440
But it's, it's not a fear unless
you're writing a business letter

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:26.580
and you're afraid to make mistakes.

00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:32.400
But by and large, the thing that seems
to, you know, generate this fear in

00:01:32.405 --> 00:01:34.920
people is, is the, is is speaking.

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:38.970
So I think there are a
number of issues there.

00:01:38.970 --> 00:01:45.824
Number one, uh, we need to be prepared
to speak and as you know, if you follow

00:01:45.824 --> 00:01:51.285
me here, that means getting a lot of
the language in you listening, reading,

00:01:51.524 --> 00:01:54.824
even using our mini stories, which
is, you know, these are stories with

00:01:54.830 --> 00:02:00.495
a lot of repetition of high frequency
verbs, common structures in the

00:02:00.495 --> 00:02:02.265
language, common words in the language.

00:02:02.505 --> 00:02:06.495
You wash these over yourself so
that you are familiar with them.

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:09.554
You at least potentially have
words that you can use because

00:02:09.554 --> 00:02:13.755
if you don't have the vocabulary,
you're, you've got nowhere to go.

00:02:13.755 --> 00:02:13.934
There...

00:02:13.934 --> 00:02:17.744
you've got no weapons, nothing
that you can find to actually

00:02:17.744 --> 00:02:19.154
maintain a conversation.

00:02:19.484 --> 00:02:23.415
And if you haven't invested a lot
of time in a lot of listening,

00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:25.170
even more so than reading.

00:02:25.170 --> 00:02:29.010
Reading, as I've said before,
helps you acquire vocabulary, but

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:33.030
listening is developing your ability
to understand what people say.

00:02:33.210 --> 00:02:35.970
If you can't understand what
people say, you're gonna have

00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:37.680
an awful time in a conversation.

00:02:37.770 --> 00:02:43.110
So the first thing in order not to
feel afraid in a conversation is to

00:02:43.110 --> 00:02:48.345
be prepared and when I, when people
say that they can read, but they can't

00:02:48.345 --> 00:02:53.535
speak, but they only ever read short
newspaper articles, that's not the same.

00:02:53.894 --> 00:03:01.515
I read, for example, even with my limited
exposure to, uh, Polish, I read this whole

00:03:01.515 --> 00:03:06.285
book without the benefit of LingQ without
being able to look up any words because I

00:03:06.285 --> 00:03:10.670
have acquired so many words through LingQ
that there's only a small percentage,

00:03:10.670 --> 00:03:15.770
relatively small percentage of words
in here that I don't know, so this is

00:03:15.770 --> 00:03:19.280
developing my confidence in the language.

00:03:19.635 --> 00:03:23.295
So obviously if you're gonna speak
comfortably, you have to be confident that

00:03:23.295 --> 00:03:25.395
you can understand what people are saying.

00:03:25.515 --> 00:03:29.385
You have to have words that
you can slowly try to activate.

00:03:29.985 --> 00:03:32.835
If you do those things,
you should be less afraid.

00:03:33.675 --> 00:03:37.245
I also don't think there's any
particular need to start speaking

00:03:37.455 --> 00:03:39.315
any earlier than you need to.

00:03:39.465 --> 00:03:43.215
Any activity that has you involved
with the language listening and

00:03:43.215 --> 00:03:46.035
reading or writing is all helping you.

00:03:46.665 --> 00:03:50.894
There should be no sort of pressure
to speak, in my opinion, and there

00:03:50.894 --> 00:03:55.155
is far too much pressure on people
to speak and to speak correctly.

00:03:55.545 --> 00:03:58.905
When I was in Poland recently,
I was speaking, I was making

00:03:58.910 --> 00:04:00.105
all kinds of mistakes.

00:04:00.315 --> 00:04:01.424
It doesn't bother me.

00:04:01.545 --> 00:04:05.805
Another reason it doesn't bother me
is because to me, when I'm speaking

00:04:05.805 --> 00:04:09.915
with someone in a language that I
don't speak at all well, like Polish.

00:04:11.090 --> 00:04:15.390
My purpose is to try to somehow
communicate, to try to understand

00:04:15.390 --> 00:04:21.539
what the other person is saying, and
also to trigger meaningful input.

00:04:22.050 --> 00:04:25.590
Because if I'm in a conversation, I
want to know what that person is saying.

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:28.789
If I'm listening to something, I can
fade and not listen or not focus.

00:04:29.050 --> 00:04:32.610
But if you're in a conversation with
someone, you're very much focused

00:04:32.610 --> 00:04:33.930
on what that person is saying.

00:04:34.050 --> 00:04:36.990
You're trying very hard to
understand what they're saying.

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:41.040
You're focused, there's a
certain white heat there of

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:43.290
attention paid to the language.

00:04:43.470 --> 00:04:47.940
And so the speaking opportunity for
me is on the one hand, a chance to

00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:51.870
practice my speaking, a chance to
become aware of where my gaps are,

00:04:51.875 --> 00:04:56.770
where my faults are, but also I'm
generating very meaningful content.

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:03.630
So as long as I'm achieving those goals,
I'm trying to speak, I'm becoming aware

00:05:03.635 --> 00:05:05.580
of my shortcomings and my problems.

00:05:05.910 --> 00:05:08.460
I'm generating meaningful content.

00:05:08.910 --> 00:05:10.050
I'm communicating.

00:05:10.050 --> 00:05:11.670
I'm doing all of those things.

00:05:12.225 --> 00:05:13.485
Then I'm satisfied.

00:05:13.515 --> 00:05:18.765
It doesn't matter to me if I don't do
as well as I would like to do, because

00:05:18.765 --> 00:05:23.745
I know that if I continue doing this
and if I continue my input activities,

00:05:23.985 --> 00:05:29.565
I will only improve and I will
improve as quickly as I can improve.

00:05:29.655 --> 00:05:31.115
I can't force myself to improve.

00:05:31.845 --> 00:05:36.705
I can only commit to continuing
the process of listening and

00:05:36.705 --> 00:05:40.155
reading, but also the process of
speaking without being afraid of it.

00:05:40.784 --> 00:05:45.854
Without assuming that someone is judging
me and, and even if they have trouble

00:05:45.854 --> 00:05:50.625
understanding me at first, or if my
pronunciation isn't very good, I have

00:05:50.625 --> 00:05:54.914
to assume that if I keep doing it and
if I keep mixing it with listening and

00:05:54.919 --> 00:06:00.255
reading, I will gradually improve, and
therefore, as long as I com I'm committed

00:06:00.255 --> 00:06:04.995
to the long-term process, how I do at
a particular point in the process like

00:06:04.995 --> 00:06:09.554
today versus three months from now versus
six months from now, how I do right now.

00:06:10.095 --> 00:06:11.475
It doesn't matter to me.

00:06:11.805 --> 00:06:17.535
And I, I'm wondering if people who are
afraid of speaking either lack patience

00:06:17.955 --> 00:06:23.265
or lack humility and maybe with a
stronger dose of patience, recognizing

00:06:23.265 --> 00:06:28.495
that the pr the brain learns, but it
learns slowly and that no one says that

00:06:28.495 --> 00:06:30.825
you have to be an all-star performer.

00:06:31.245 --> 00:06:31.575
That's...

00:06:31.575 --> 00:06:34.455
you are not promised that you're
gonna be an all-star performer.

00:06:35.115 --> 00:06:37.095
Uh, you are learning for your own reasons.

00:06:37.425 --> 00:06:41.385
If you are enjoying learning, then
you should also enjoy the process of

00:06:41.385 --> 00:06:46.395
speaking, albeit with mistakes with
less than optimal, uh, pronunciation.

00:06:46.665 --> 00:06:51.375
See it as an opportunity to keep
improving, you know, a further step

00:06:51.375 --> 00:06:55.305
in the journey towards where you
eventually wanna get to, which is a

00:06:55.305 --> 00:06:56.835
comfortable level in the language.

00:06:56.865 --> 00:07:02.115
So, speaking is one of the sort of
four major skills in language learning.

00:07:02.115 --> 00:07:03.615
It's a major activity.

00:07:03.795 --> 00:07:05.925
It's not one we should be afraid of.

00:07:06.315 --> 00:07:08.925
It's one that we should
do when we want to do it.

00:07:09.315 --> 00:07:14.325
We shouldn't, in a way, jump in there
too quickly if we're not comfortable.

00:07:14.655 --> 00:07:19.125
On the other hand, we shouldn't use the
fear of making a mistake as a reason

00:07:19.125 --> 00:07:25.620
for not speaking so make sure you
wanna speak when you wanna speak, start

00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:27.570
speaking and don't second guess yourself.

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:32.220
There you have it, the sort of role
of speaking in language acquisition

00:07:32.225 --> 00:07:34.650
as seen from my perspective.

00:07:34.920 --> 00:07:35.880
Thank you for listening.

00:07:35.910 --> 00:07:36.330
Bye for now.

